- **Necessity, for every k.** Any forest that can be solved in k tests satisfies a ⪯_w N(k). The prefix sums of N(k) obey S_{N(k)}(t) = S_{N(k−1)}(t) + max over m of [S_{N(k−1)}(m) + S_{N(k−1)}(t−m)]. This matches what one test can do: the three answer classes each need to be solvable in k−1 tests.
- **Sufficiency for k ≤ 3.** An exact recursive solver confirmed that every list ⪯_w N(k) can be solved in k tests.
- **Sufficiency for k = 4.** Since smaller or split-up stars never make the problem harder, only lists of length 16 with sum 81 need checking. I enumerated them exhaustively (5,997,038 lists; at most 408,776 checked in a single run). Every one splits into three classes each ⪯_w N(3), so none fails.

**What is only tested, not proven:**
- **k = 5.** Checking one level down is exact here, because k = 4 is fully verified. The test search is randomized, so a missed list is not proof of a failure. Two exceptions:
  - The 16-ones run counted 228M lists. It had no unresolved lists, so every one was confirmed solvable.
  - The one unresolved list from the flat-top family was solved exactly by an integer program, and I checked that each of its three classes is ⪯_w N(4).

  None of the following families produced a failure:
  - about 200k uniform random lists;
  - about 820k lists close to N(5);
  - 7.88M lists with a nearly flat top and a small tail;
  - 100k lists with a tight top;
  - two-value families;
  - about 228M lists with exactly 16 trailing 1s;
  - about 300M lists with exactly 8 trailing 1s. This run was stopped before it finished.
- **k = 6 and 7.** Random samples and two-value families also found no failure.

The k = 5 space has about 1.4×10¹² candidate lists, so these runs cover only part of it. If a counterexample exists, it is at k ≥ 5 and outside the families I tested.

$$\boxed{+\infty}$$

